<BlogLayout
  title="AI websites aren’t automatically slop. Lazy briefs are."
  seoTitle="AI Websites Aren't Slop When Structure Is Right"
  description="Google doesn’t care whether AI helped build your trade website. It cares about structure, relevance, and speed. Here’s how we use AI without shipping slop."
  date="September 2026"
  datePublished="2026-09-01"
  readTime="5 min"
>
  <p>
    Yes: if you type “build me a beautiful SEO website” into a chatbot and ship the first draft,
    you’ll get slop. That says nothing about whether
    <a href="/services/websites">AI-assisted builds</a> can outperform slow page-builder workflows
    when you already know what Google and homeowners need.
  </p>

  <h2>What Google actually cares about</h2>
  <p>
    Fundamentals: clear relevance for the jobs people search, trustworthy proof, usable pages, fast
    loads, honest service-area architecture. The CMS or generator is irrelevant. A carefully
    structured AI-built site and a carefully structured hand-coded site play the same game.
  </p>

  <h2>Where AI wins for trades</h2>
  <p>
    Speed. Once keywords and page framework are locked (hero, proof, services, areas, process,
    stakes, FAQ), AI drafts and iterates in a fraction of the WordPress timeline, including new
    service-area pages. That’s how a company like
    <a href="/blog/dry-duck-launch">Dry Duck</a> could launch on a tiny budget without waiting six
    months for a brochure site.
  </p>

  <h2>What we never outsource to the model</h2>
  <p>
    Keyword strategy against real competitors. The conversion framework. QA for broken flows and
    performance. “Make it look cool” without that spine is how AI earned its bad reputation. With
    the spine, refusing AI is mostly nostalgia.
  </p>
</BlogLayout>
